## Proof

**Strategy.** We exhibit three distinct integers $x < y < z$ in $[n, 2n]$ whose three pairwise sums are all perfect squares. With two colors and three integers, the pigeonhole principle forces two to share a color; their sum is a square, giving the required $a, b$.

**Construction.** For an integer $k \geq 9$, set
$$x = 2k(k-2), \qquad y = 2k^2+1, \qquad z = 2k(k+2).$$

Then the pairwise sums are
$$x+y = 4k^2-4k+1 = (2k-1)^2, \qquad y+z = 4k^2+4k+1 = (2k+1)^2, \qquad x+z = 4k^2 = (2k)^2,$$
all perfect squares. Since $-4k < 1 < 4k$ for $k\geq 1$, we have $x < y < z$, so the three values are distinct.

**Range check.** We need $x \geq n$ and $z \leq 2n$, i.e.
$$2k(k-2) \geq n \quad\text{and}\quad 2k(k+2) \leq 2n,$$
which is equivalent to
$$k(k+2) \leq n \leq 2k(k-2). \tag{$\star$}$$
Once $(\star)$ holds, $x \geq n$ and $z \leq 2n$; since $x < y < z$, we also get $y \in [n, 2n]$ automatically.

**Existence of suitable $k$ for every $n \geq 99$.** Let $f(k)=k(k+2)$ and $g(k)=2k(k-2)$. For $k \geq 6$ we have $f(k) \leq g(k)$. Consider the intervals $I_k = [f(k),\, g(k)]$ for $k \geq 9$:

- $f(9) = 9 \cdot 11 = 99$, so $I_9$ starts at $99$.
- $g(k) = 2k^2-4k \to \infty$, so the union of intervals is unbounded above.
- Consecutive intervals overlap: $g(k) \geq f(k+1)$ iff $2k(k-2) \geq (k+1)(k+3)$ iff $k^2 - 8k - 3 \geq 0$, which holds for all $k \geq 9$ (since $9^2 - 72 - 3 = 6 \geq 0$).

Therefore $\bigcup_{k \geq 9} I_k = [99, \infty)$, and for every $n \geq 99$ there exists $k \geq 9$ satisfying $(\star)$.

**Conclusion.** Given any two-coloring of the non-negative integers and any $n \geq 99$, choose $k \geq 9$ satisfying $(\star)$, and form $x < y < z$ as above. All three lie in $[n, 2n]$, and each pairwise sum is a perfect square. By the pigeonhole principle, two of $\{x, y, z\}$ receive the same color. Naming them $a < b$, we obtain $n \leq a < b \leq 2n$, with $a, b$ of the same color and $a + b$ a perfect square. $\blacksquare$

